.TH KOSH 5 "2026-10-07" "kosh" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
kosh.conf \- Koshka settings files and the KOSHCONF encoding
.SH DESCRIPTION
The settings Koshka owns live in koshconf files. They hold option values, not
shell commands. A shell loads them before any shell startup file, whether or
not it is interactive. The following do not read a settings file or apply
KOSHCONF: a shell started with
.B \-Q
or
.BR \-\-no\-config ,
a lint, format, or language server run, rescue mode, a shell whose real and
effective ids differ, and a fresh evaluator that receives its state from its
parent. A binary started as a koshkit utility or as
.B koshkit
also does not read a settings file or apply KOSHCONF.
Privileged mode,
.BR \-p ,
loads the files but ignores KOSHCONF.
.PP
The configured mood also applies to
.B kosh \-c
and to a script without a shebang, so a file that sets
.B mood=bash
runs such code under the bash grammar. Name the mood with
.B \-\-mood
or a shebang where the grammar matters.
The
.B koshconf
builtin in
.BR kosh (1)
loads, changes, and writes these settings.
.PP
Shell code that should run at startup belongs in the startup files of the
.B bash
or
.B sh
mood listed under FILES. The
.B kosh
mood sources no shell startup file. The init_moods option lists the
moods whose startup files an interactive or login shell sources, so a
.B kosh
mood session can still source ~/.bashrc; see STARTUP MOODS.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /etc/kosh.conf " or " %ProgramData%\kosh\kosh.conf
The system settings file, read first. Because it applies to every user, it is
read only when nobody else could have written it. On POSIX the open file and
its directory must be owned by root and must not be writable by their group or
by others; the file is checked on the descriptor that is then read, so it
cannot be swapped between the check and the read. On Windows the ProgramData
directory comes from the system's known folder, not from an environment
variable, and the file and its
.I kosh
directory must be owned by SYSTEM, Administrators, or TrustedInstaller. No
access control entry on the file may let any other account write, append,
delete, change the permissions of, or take ownership of it. No entry on the
directory may let another account delete its entries, delete or rename it,
change its permissions, or take ownership of it. Replacing an existing file
needs one of those rights, so the right to add files and subdirectories, which
the default ProgramData permissions pass to every user, is allowed on the
directory; a file another account adds is owned by that account and fails the
owner check. An entry that applies only to children, such as the usual CREATOR
OWNER entry, is not counted. A file that fails the check is skipped with a
warning that explains the reason and, on POSIX, suggests the
.B chown root
or
.B chmod go\-w
command that would let the shell trust it.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf " or " ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
The user settings file, read second. XDG_CONFIG_HOME is used when it is set to
an absolute path.
.BR "koshconf create" ,
.BR "koshconf set \-\-persist" ,
and
.B koshconf save \-\-persist
write this file. When the path is a symbolic link, as a dotfile manager such as
stow leaves it, the link is kept and its final target is written instead. The
new text goes to a temporary file in the target's directory. The file is flushed
to disk and renamed over the target. A reader sees either the old or the new
file. The directory is created when it is missing. The written file keeps the
permission bits of the file it replaces, and a new file is created with mode
0644 less the file creation mask. Each write holds an advisory lock on the
target's directory while it loads and replaces the file, so concurrent
.B koshconf set \-\-persist
commands that change different options all take effect. On Windows the lock is
a hidden
.I .kosh\-flock.lock
file in that directory, and only the owner write bit of the mode is kept.
.TP
.I /etc/profile
The system login file of the
.B bash
and
.B sh
moods.
.TP
.I ~/.profile
The user POSIX login file and the last Bash login candidate.
.TP
.I ~/.bash_profile
The first user Bash login candidate.
.TP
.I ~/.bash_login
The second user Bash login candidate.
.TP
.IR /etc/bash/bashrc " or " /etc/bash.bashrc
The first readable path is the system rc file of an interactive non-login Bash
mood.
.TP
.I ~/.bashrc
The default user rc file of an interactive non-login Bash mood.
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion
Bash programmable completion, sourced after the Bash startup chain sources an rc
file. It is skipped when completion is disabled, when a default completion
specification exists, and when BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO is already set.
.PP
The
.B \-\-rcfile
and
.B \-\-init\-file
options and the ENV and BASH_ENV variables may select a different shell startup
file in the
.B bash
and
.B sh
moods. See
.BR kosh (1)
for their conditions and expansion rules.
.SH SYNTAX
A settings file is a sequence of lines. LF and CRLF end a line. A UTF-8 byte
order mark at the start of the file is skipped. A line that is empty, holds only
blanks, or starts with a number sign after optional blanks is ignored.
.PP
Every other line has the form
.IR name = value .
Blanks around the name and around the value are ignored, so
.B "name = on"
is the same as
.BR name=on .
A value that starts with a double or a single quote ends at the next quote of
the same kind, and only blanks may follow it; quote a value whose own blanks
at either end must be kept.
Otherwise the value is everything between the blanks after the equals sign and
the blanks at the end of the line. A value cannot span lines.
.PP
A boolean is written as on or off and is also read as true, false, 1, or 0. An
enumeration is written by name. A string is taken as written, and must be valid
UTF-8 without a NUL byte. editor.history.max_entries is a decimal integer from
0 to 2147483647. init_moods is a comma-separated list of the moods that
.B \-L
accepts, such as kosh, sh, bash, and bash-posix. An empty name is skipped.
.B koshconf
quotes a written value that starts with a quote or starts or ends with a blank.
A value containing a newline cannot be written to a file. A value containing
both quote characters cannot be written if the value needs quoting.
.B koshconf set \-\-persist
rejects either value before changing the session.
.B koshconf list
prints one name=value line for each option, without a class column, and shows
such a value, and any value with a control byte or invalid UTF-8, in the
.B $\(aq...\(aq
form of the shell.
.PP
A file may set every option except the ones fixed by how the shell started,
such as interpreter.bash.login_shell, interpreter.bash.restricted_shell, interpreter.bash.privileged_mode,
interpreter.posix.parse_without_executing, and interpreter.bash.exit_after_one_command, and the ones
that follow the session, interpreter.posix.job_control, editor.history.bash.records_commands,
editor.history.bash.bang_expansion, and interpreter.bash.posixly_correct. The OPTION REGISTRY
section of
.BR kosh (1)
lists them.
.PP
.B A semantic option in a file changes every shell that loads it. This includes
.B the interactive session and every script that kosh runs.
For example, interpreter.posix.exit_on_command_failure=on makes every script exit on its
first failing command, and interpreter.bash.glob_includes_dotfiles=on changes what every
glob matches. Put such a setting in a file only when every script should run
with it; otherwise set it for a session with
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
or
.BR "koshconf set" .
.PP
The mood is applied first, wherever it appears in the file, so the other lines
are read under it. In the
.B kosh
mood, interpreter.posix.unset_variable_is_error, interpreter.posix.pipeline_fails_on_any_stage,
interpreter.bash.glob_no_match_is_error, and interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers stay
on and interpreter.bash.glob_no_match_expands_to_nothing stays off; a file that sets
another value gets a warning that identifies the file, the line, the option,
and
.BR "koshconf set mood bash" ,
and the line is skipped. An option specified on the command line, such as
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR +e ,
.BR "\-o pipefail" ,
.BR "+O extglob" ,
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-\-no\-traces ,
or
.BR \-L ,
keeps the command-line value, so
.B kosh +e script
runs without errexit whatever a file says. In the same way, a KOSH_HISTORY_FILE
or KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE variable inherited from the environment keeps its value
over editor.history.file_path or editor.history.max_entries. To run with no
settings file at all, start the shell with
.BR \-\-no\-config .
A later line replaces an earlier one, and the
user file and KOSHCONF replace the system file.
.PP
The canonical name of an option inherited from POSIX
.B set \-o
carries a posix segment before its last part, and one inherited from any other
Bash
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
option carries a bash segment there, as in
interpreter.posix.exit_on_command_failure for errexit and
interpreter.bash.glob_extended_patterns for extglob. A name without either
segment belongs to kosh alone.
.PP
Every option that has a Bash spelling is also accepted under an alias. The
alias is legacy.posix.NAME when POSIX
.B set \-o
accepts NAME. Otherwise it is legacy.bash.NAME when
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
accepts NAME. Examples include legacy.bash.extglob, legacy.bash.failglob,
legacy.bash.braceexpand, and legacy.bash.posix. An alias resolves to the same
option entry as the canonical name, with the same id and the same storage, so
setting either spelling changes the value shown by the other spelling,
.BR "set \-o" ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR $\- ,
and SHELLOPTS. It is a second spelling resolved during lookup. A kosh.conf
line and the koshconf set and koshconf get commands accept the alias, as does
every other option-name operand.
A line that
sets an option twice through both spellings follows the rule above that a later
line replaces an earlier one.
.B koshconf create
and
.BR "koshconf list" ,
error messages, and KOSHCONF use only the canonical name.
.B koshconf list \-\-all
is the exception: it prints the alias of each option on a line after the
canonical line, with the same value.
.PP
.B koshconf create
writes every option a file may hold, with a blank line between topics, each
after a
.RI # " help"
comment that also identifies its
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
spelling. A string option without a default, such as editor.history.file_path,
is written commented out. The
.B bash
preset also writes interpreter.bash.aliases_expand commented out, because bash expands
aliases in an interactive shell and not in a script, and a written value would
apply to both. The
.B kosh
preset writes the values the
.B kosh
mood keeps, so reading it back never warns.
.SH STARTUP MOODS
init_moods lists the moods whose startup files an interactive or login
shell sources, in order, with each file parsed under the grammar of its mood.
It is empty by default. An empty value sources the files of the session mood,
so it sources no files in the
.B kosh
mood. It is the configured form of
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-L
on the command line replaces it. A shell that is neither interactive nor a
login shell ignores it, so scripts keep their own startup rules. A restricted
shell ignores it with a warning, as it does the mood, because it would choose
which files run before the restrictions take effect. KOSHCONF never carries
it. Changing it with
.B koshconf set
in a running shell sources nothing; use
.B set \-L
for that.
.PP
To keep the
.B kosh
mood for the session and still source ~/.bashrc, write
.PP
.RS
.EX
init_moods=bash
.EE
.RE
.PP
in the user file. Every mood sets KOSH and never exports it, so a line in
~/.bashrc guarded with
.B test \-z \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in another shell, and one guarded with
.B test \-n \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in Koshka:
.PP
.RS
.EX
test \-z "$KOSH" && source /usr/share/bash\-completion/bash_completion
test \-n "$KOSH" && alias ll='ls \-l'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Listing bash also sets BASH_VERSION in that session, as
.B \-L bash
does, so a ~/.bashrc that checks it proceeds.
.SH KOSHCONF
.B KOSHCONF
is a dynamic shell variable that always holds the current settings in a binary
form, encoded as standard base64 with padding. It is never exported by itself.
After
.BR "export KOSHCONF" ,
each command the shell starts receives the settings current when it starts,
not a copy taken at the export. Another shell receives it through
.B koshconf load
.I value
or through a KOSHCONF variable in its environment.
.B koshconf save
prints the current form, so
.B KOSHCONF=$(koshconf save)
starts a kosh with the settings of the shell that ran it, and
.B koshconf save \-\-persist
writes the current settings to the user settings file instead. The variable is applied after
the settings files and removed before any command runs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ssh host KOSHCONF="$KOSHCONF" kosh
.EE
.RE
.PP
The decoded bytes are a sequence of records. Each record is an option id, a
value length, and the value. The id and the length are unsigned LEB128 varints:
seven bits per byte, least significant group first, with the high bit set on
every byte but the last. A boolean value is one byte, 0 or 1. An enumeration is
a varint holding the value's position in its list. A string is its UTF-8 bytes.
The ids are listed by
.BR kosh (1)
and never change meaning. Only the mood and the interactive options are
written. An interactive option affects only the interactive editor, the
history, or how diagnostics are presented, never how a script runs. A child
shell loads the settings files itself. Passing semantic options such as
interpreter.posix.exit_on_command_failure, interpreter.mimic_shebang, or the debug
reports through the environment would change how every script below the shell
runs. Bash exports SHELLOPTS only on request.
.PP
A reader skips a record with an unknown id, an option it never carries, or a
value that a settings file would reject, and reports a warning. Only the
canonical form is accepted: the base64 text must carry its padding, every
varint
must use the fewest bytes, and the ids must strictly ascend in writer order. A
blob that breaks any of these rules, or whose records are truncated, is rejected
as a whole with a warning that identifies the broken rule.
.SH ERRORS
A missing settings file is skipped. An unreadable one, and a system file that
fails the ownership check under FILES, produce a warning. A line
without an equals sign, an unknown option name, an invalid value, an unclosed
quote, text after a closing quote, and an option a file cannot hold each
produce a warning that identifies the file and line, and the line is skipped.
The warning for an unknown option name suggests a close option name when one
exists.
The warning renders a control byte or invalid UTF-8 byte from the file as an
escape
in its message and as a question mark in the source excerpt. A settings file
never stops the shell.
.PP
Shell startup files produce the ordinary shell diagnostics described in
.BR kosh (1).
.SH MIGRATION
Earlier releases read /etc/koshrc and ~/.koshrc as shell code in the
.B kosh
mood. They are no longer read, and an interactive shell in the
.B kosh
mood prints one warning naming them when either exists. A non-interactive shell
and a shell started with
.B \-Q
stay silent. Move option settings into
.I kosh.conf
with
.B "koshconf set"
.I option value
.BR \-\-persist ,
and move shell code into ~/.bashrc with
.BR mood=bash ,
with
.BR init_moods=bash ,
or with
.B \-L bash
on the command line. Guard Koshka-only lines with
.B test \-n \(dq$KOSH\(dq
and lines for other shells with
.BR "test \-z \(dq$KOSH\(dq" .
KOSH is set in every mood and never exported.
.SH EXAMPLES
A user file that keeps the bash grammar, edits with vi keys, and allows patterns
match dotfiles in every script:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mood=bash

editor.auto_close_brackets_and_quotes=on
completion.menu_style=plain

editor.history.max_entries=20000

editor.base_mode=vi

interpreter.bash.glob_includes_dotfiles=on
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same lines are written by
.B koshconf set
.I option value
.BR \-\-persist ,
and
.B grep glob ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
finds every globbing setting in a file
.B koshconf create
wrote.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (1)
